    hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor reader(buffer);

    // Read message_type (uint16_t)
    header.message_type = reader.read<uint16_t>();

    // Read body_length (uint32_t)
    header.body_length = reader.read<uint32_t>();

    // Skip the body_data based on body_length
    reader.skip(header.body_length);

    // Read footer (uint32_t)
    header.footer = reader.read<uint32_t>();

    return header;
}
```

The implementation:
1. Creates a `hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor` from the input span to manage reading with a moving cursor
2. Uses `read<uint16_t>()` to read the message type
3. Uses `read<uint32_t>()` to read the body length
4. Uses `skip()` to advance the cursor past the body data without reading it
5. Uses `read<uint32_t>()` to read the footer value
6. Returns the populated `MessageHeader` struct

This follows the PMC (Pointer-Moving Cursor) pattern shown in the code fragments, where the reader maintains an internal position that advances as data is read or skipped.

