output.text:
Looking at the code fragments and the Hexi library's `intrusive_storage` interface, I can see the available methods and implement the required functions. Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/detail/intrusive_storage.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <algorithm>

template<size_t block_size = 1024>
std::vector<uint8_t> preview_data_without_consuming(const hexi::detail::intrusive_storage<block_size>& storage, size_t bytes_to_preview) {
    // Get the readable data span without consuming it
    auto read_data = storage.read_span();

    // Limit preview size to available data
    size_t actual_preview_size = std::min(bytes_to_preview, read_data.size());

    // Copy data to vector without advancing read pointer
    std::vector<uint8_t> preview_buffer(actual_preview_size);
    std::copy_n(read_data.begin(), actual_preview_size, preview_buffer.begin());

    return preview_buffer;
}

template<size_t block_size = 1024>
struct BufferStatus {
    size_t readable_size;
    size_t free_space;
};

template<size_t block_size>
BufferStatus<block_size> check_buffer_status(const hexi::detail::intrusive_storage<block_size>& storage) {
    BufferStatus<block_size> status;

    // Get the size of readable data
    status.readable_size = storage.size();

    // Get the amount of free space
    status.free_space = storage.free();

    return status;
}

// Specialization for default block_size
BufferStatus<> check_buffer_status(const hexi::detail::intrusive_storage<1024>& storage) {
    BufferStatus<> status;

    // Get the size of readable data
    status.readable_size = storage.size();

    // Get the amount of free space
    status.free_space = storage.free();

    return status;
}

template<size_t block_size = 1024>
void reset_buffer(hexi::detail::intrusive_storage<block_size>& storage) {
    // Reset both read and write offsets to beginning
    storage.reset();
}
```

The implementation:

1. **`preview_data_without_consuming`**: Uses `read_span()` to get a view of the readable data without modifying the read pointer. It copies the requested number of bytes (or less if not enough data is available) into a vector and returns it.

2. **`check_buffer_status`**: Returns a `BufferStatus` struct containing:
   - `readable_size`: obtained via `storage.size()` which returns the number of bytes available to read
   - `free_space`: obtained via `storage.free()` which returns the number of bytes of free space

3. **`reset_buffer`**: Calls `storage.reset()` to reset both read and write offsets to their initial state.

