output.text:
Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides:

1. `binary_stream_writer` has a `serialise()` method that takes an object with a `serialise(auto& stream)` method
2. `binary_stream_reader` has a `deserialise()` method that takes an object with a `serialise(auto& stream)` method
3. Both use stream adaptors internally to handle the serialization/deserialization

The key insight is that the same `serialise()` method on the custom object is used for both serialization and deserialization - the stream adaptor determines the direction.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct UserData {
    uint32_t user_id;
    std::string name;

    void serialise(auto& stream) {
        stream << user_id << name;
    }
};

void serialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const UserData& data) {
    // Use the writer's serialise method to serialize the object
    // We need to cast away const since serialise() expects a non-const reference
    // but for writing, it won't modify the object
    writer.serialise(const_cast<UserData&>(data));
}

void deserialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader, UserData& data) {
    // Use the reader's deserialise method to deserialize the object
    reader.deserialise(data);
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`serialize_object`**: Uses `writer.serialise(data)` which internally creates a `stream_write_adaptor` and calls `data.serialise(adaptor)`. The adaptor handles writing the data to the stream. Note: We need to cast away `const` because the `serialise()` method signature expects a non-const reference (to support both read and write operations with the same method).

2. **`deserialize_object`**: Uses `reader.deserialise(data)` which internally creates a `stream_read_adaptor` and calls `data.serialise(adaptor)`. The adaptor handles reading the data from the stream and populating the object.

The beauty of this design is that the same `serialise()` method in `UserData` works for both directions - the stream adaptor type determines whether data is being written or read.

